{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And the days of Israel drew near to die.</b> From where did he know? Rather, he recognized in himself that his powers had faded, and he wished to charge Joseph, but he was not ill; and afterward he fell ill, and it was told to Joseph, 'Behold, your father is ill.' And so we find with David (1 Kings 2:1): 'And the days of David drew near to die' — for he perceived this in himself, and from this he said (ibid.), 'Behold, I am going the way of all the earth.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): the reason he mentioned 'Israel' and did not say 'the days of Jacob to die,' as he said at first, 'And Jacob lived' and 'the days of Jacob were' — from the time the Holy One, blessed be He, called him by the name Israel and said to him (Genesis 32:29), 'Your name shall no more be called Jacob, but Israel,' the pericopes follow this practice, sometimes calling him Israel and sometimes Jacob. For what He said, 'Your name shall no more be called Jacob, but Israel' — this is not a removal, but rather that the name Israel is primary and the name Jacob subordinate to it. This pericope too follows this practice: 'And it was told to Jacob, saying, Behold, your son Joseph,' and 'And Israel strengthened himself.' And it further says, 'And Jacob said to Joseph, El Shaddai,' and 'And Israel saw Joseph's sons.' And it is further written at the end of the pericope, 'And Jacob finished commanding his sons, and the physicians embalmed Israel.'" "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): the reason the verses mention in this pericope sometimes Jacob, sometimes Israel — all of it is by providence and with a known intention. For the name Jacob is said concerning the traits of the body in bodily matters in this world, for he was called Jacob on account of (Genesis 25): 'and his hand took hold of Esau's heel,' and the name Israel is said concerning the traits of the soul, as it is written (Genesis 32): 'for you have striven with Elohim.'" "<b>And it is known</b> that the principal intention is in the traits of the soul and not in the traits of the body. Nevertheless, it is impossible for a person to uproot the traits of the body entirely and not use them at all, for he cannot live without them; but the intention is that the soul be primary and the traits of the body subordinate. And this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, said: not that the name Jacob is uprooted from its place, but that Israel is primary and Jacob subordinate to it. And whoever makes the traits of the body primary and the traits of the soul — which is the worship of HaShem, blessed be He — subordinate, behold, he puts his soul to death. And from this David said (Psalms 22:30): 'All the fat ones of the earth shall eat and bow down; all who go down to the dust shall kneel before Him — and his soul did not live.' He called 'the fat ones of the earth' those who are drawn after desires, for they are the ones who hold to the trait of the body and make it primary and make the worship of HaShem, blessed be He, subordinate. And from this he mentioned first 'they shall eat' and afterward 'and bow down'; 'before Him shall kneel all who go down to the dust' — this kneeling is not an expression of worship but an expression of lowering, from the language of (Psalms 20:9) 'they bowed down and fell,' that is, they shall be punished and descend to the dust, because not one of them strives to keep his soul alive. And so the way of the verses is to mention Jacob concerning material matters, the composite bodily things, and Israel concerning the simple, refined, pure, and spiritual things. And this is the language of the prophet who said (Isaiah 43:1): 'Your Creator, Jacob, and Your Former, Israel.' He mentioned 'creating' alongside Jacob and 'forming' alongside Israel. And so the verses attribute 'creation' to darkness and 'formation' to light, as it is written (Isaiah 45:7): 'Former of light and Creator of darkness.' And likewise they attribute men to Israel and women to Jacob, as it is written (Exodus 19:3): 'Thus shall you say to the house of Jacob and tell the children of Israel.' And this is the saying of the prophet (Isaiah 43:22): 'But you have not called upon Me, Jacob, for you have wearied yourself with Me, Israel.' He says: when you are Jacob and drawn after matter and bodily affairs, you have not called upon Me; but when you weary yourself with Me and strive in the affairs of the soul, you are called Israel. And therefore the pericope began, 'And Jacob lived,' and said, 'And the days of Jacob were,' for concerning the life of the body in this world the name Jacob was necessary of necessity; and he mentioned the name Israel concerning death, for the death of the body in this world is the life of the soul in the world to come. And likewise, 'And Israel said to Joseph, Behold, I am dying,' and likewise, 'And Israel bowed,' and 'And Israel strengthened himself' — whenever he mentions the bed upon which he dies, he mentions it with the name Israel. And likewise at the end of the pericope he returned to his first matter and mentioned Jacob, as it says, 'And Jacob finished commanding his sons,' and after he mentioned, 'And he gathered his feet into the bed,' he mentioned him by the name Israel: 'And the physicians embalmed Israel.' And what is written, 'And it was told to Jacob, saying, Behold, your son Joseph' — for 'these are the generations of Jacob: Joseph,' and when Joseph was born, Jacob was his name and he was not yet called by the name Israel. And likewise, 'And Jacob said to Joseph, El Shaddai, etc.,' for at the beginning of this vision that was in Luz, Jacob was his name, as it is written (Genesis 35:6): 'And Jacob came to Luz.' And from there onward it mentions Israel every time. And the reason he mentioned, 'And Jacob called to his sons,' and did not say, 'And Israel called to his sons,' is because the Shekhinah departed from him. For the mighty sovereignty in the ascent of the soul is the vision of the face of the Shekhinah." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'And the days of Israel drew near to die' — this is what the verse says (1 Chronicles 29:15): 'For we are strangers before You, and sojourners, as all our fathers were; like a shadow are our days upon the earth, and there is no hope.' Would that it were like the shadow of a wall or the shadow of a tree! Rather, like the shadow of a bird flying in the air, as it is said (Psalms 144:4): 'His days are like a passing shadow' — the bird passes and its shadow passes with it; therefore it says, 'His days are like a passing shadow.' You should say: 'like a shadow are our days upon the earth.' 'And there is no hope' — there is none whom one may hope will not die; they all know and say with their mouths that they die. Abraham said (Genesis 15:2): 'And I am going childless.' Isaac said (ibid. 27:4): 'in order that my soul may bless you before I die.' And Jacob too said, 'And I shall lie down with my fathers' — when? At the time he inclined toward death. 'And the days of Israel drew near to die' — this is what the verse says (Ecclesiastes 8:8): 'There is no man who has power over the spirit, and there is no authority on the day of death.' When Jacob inclined toward death, he began to humble himself before Joseph, saying to him, 'If now I have found favor in your eyes.' And so you find with David (1 Kings): 'And the days of David drew near to die.' Until now they called him king, as it is said (1 Kings 1:1): 'And King David was old, advanced in days.' When he inclined toward death, it did not say, 'And the days of David the king drew near to die,' but rather, 'And the days of David drew near to die' — to fulfill what is said (Ecclesiastes 8:8): 'and there is no authority on the day of death.'" "<b>And he called his son, to Joseph.</b> He did not call Reuben, who was the firstborn, nor Judah, who was the king, but rather Joseph — why? Because he had the power to do it. <b>Please do not bury me in Egypt.</b> He asked him for two things: that he not bury him in Egypt, and that he bury him in the land of Israel. That he not bury him in Egypt — because the Egyptians might make him into an idolatrous object, for just as punishment is exacted from those who worship, so too it is exacted from those who are worshipped, as it is said (Exodus 12): ״ובכל אלהי מצרים אעשה שפטים״. And that he bury him in the land of Israel — because the patriarchs desire and cherish the graves of the land of Israel, for it is a holy land, atoning for sins, in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 33): ״העם היושב בה נשוא עון״. And it is written (Deuteronomy 32): ״וכפר אדמתו עמו״. And there is there the gate of heaven for prayers and sacrifices to enter, and the souls that merit enter through there and return to their root; therefore the righteous long to die in the land of Israel, so that the soul may find the wisdom an open doorway and not need to toil and roll about; and furthermore, the dead of the land of Israel will live first at the time of the Messiah, but the dead outside the land do not live except through the suffering of rolling through tunnels."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {3 "The abbreviation הש\"י resolved to HaShem, blessed be He, following his standard usage; it may also stand for HaShem Yitbarakh.", 4 "The prooftext cited as Genesis 27:4 for Isaac's words ('in order that my soul may bless you before I die') appears in the Hebrew as (שם כ״ז), resolved as Genesis 27:4 from context; the abbreviation שם refers back to the book of Genesis last mentioned."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:25.479117486Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}